WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
 
it was a win  that unified a divided country we were  united as a  rainbow nation in 1995  one year after the country's first ever  multi-racial elections  south africa hosted and won the rugby  world cup  when the final whistle went and i looked  around the stadium  all different creeds and color people  they were  hugging each other it adds impetus  to our nation building effort  but 25 years later south african rugby  is still embroiled in the politics of  race politics can never be  separated from sport i think we are more  divided  at this stage than we were then  almost 90 percent of south africans are  black or mixed race  yet they remain the minority in the  spring box the national rugby team  in an attempt to get more black players  into the top tier of the game  south africa's government introduced  quotas to force coaches to pick black  players  but it's a controversial strategy  chester williams was one of the first  black players to ever represent the  springboks  definitely quota means to me opportunity  peter de villiers  was south africa's first black coach  i don't think this is your solution  [Music]  both grew up in townships in palm but  they feel very differently about  quotas historically rugby is supposed to  be  played only by white people  [Music]  because of apartheid only the white  people were selected for supreme mock  team  i always wanted to be a springbok myself  chester williams was the only black  player in the famous mandela  1995 world cup winning side  he inspired millions of south africans  all i wanted was the opportunity and  that i can prove to the world  that black people can also play rugby i  represent not only myself but represent  my community i represent black people in  south africa  after retiring in 2000 chester became a  coach  at the university of western cape being  a so-called black or colored  player in south africa was challenging  times  i remember vividly they stopped me from  going to have dinner because  i was the only black eye on the team and  at that time we were not allowed  to dine with the white people securing a  spot on the springbok side wasn't easy  you had to train twice as hard as the  privileged guy because  you have to prove every single time why  you need to be in a team  even though you score more tries than  anyone else  that's why chester believes that quotas  is still needed  to ensure black players get into the  national sport let's go let's go  if you don't have the quota system then  people won't change  and place one comes through the system  and in doing that  we have seen a lot of black kids getting  opportunity to play for the springbok  since the 1995 win the springboks team  has incrementally become more balanced  [Music]  and the springboks selected more black  and mixed race players than ever before  in 2018  but quotas are not always popular in a  recent poll  82 of black south africans said that  players should be picked solely on merit  [Music]  and the perception that so-called quota  players don't deserve to be selected  is damaging while i was playing i mean  people would call me a quota player  it was almost like a smack in your face  that you're not good enough to play  there but you selected only because the  color of your skin  i can assure you we will carry 50  million people  in our hearts as south africa's first  black coach  peter de villier's appointment was mired  in allegations that it was politically  motivated  they couldn't handle that somebody of  color  would be at the helm of rugby they never  thought  that it would happen the last question  of our party was rugby  so they tried to keep the game exclusive  for them  he felt the significance of his  appointment stretched far beyond sport  the overwhelmed responsibility of  getting this job  was born from the fact that we died in  this regina  um one of my friends was shot  next to me during the apartheid years  when we were fighting to  for freedom in the streets and those  things placed a  huge huge portion of pressure on my back  unlike chester peter believes that  quotas are not the way to get south  africa's rugby team  to better reflect the makeup of society  i don't think quotas is your solution  what bothers me a lot about quotas at  this moment  is the fact there's no imperial  no nothing the most ambitious target yet  has been set for 2019 that 50 percent of  springbok players appearing over the  course of the season  are black this includes the world cup  squad when i was a coach i never looked  at quotas i never looked at the race  one thing that was very important for me  was potential  one thing on which chester and peter  both agree  is that the best way to nurture  potential of black players is to do so  within their communities  i believe that we need to start it at a  younger age  it should be done at school level and at  provincial level  to make it easier for coaches to select  those players  i would like to see that coming in the  next 10 years where we say  south africa gets selected only not  because of the color of the skin but  they selected because they're south  africans  they go now to those townships find the  best talent  remove them from that place and put it  in their schools  wouldn't it be better if they upskill  those guys  where they are and at the level playing  field you can see  who really is the best  [Music]  rugby's struggle to transform is seen by  some as a metaphor for disillusionment  among black people who gain political  but not economic freedom after apartheid  i'm still surprised because uh to see  that people are still talking about  craters  because it's 25 years since unification  you know  and in sport we should have gone long  past that  there was no repentance for apartheid  so those who tried to forgive what did  they believe  who did they forgive so this  conversation  for another 25 years was still gone  [Music]  you

